    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n2028 BMW X7 M60 xDrive Appears To Feature M Yellow Lights\n14 photos\nPhoto: SH Proshots\nThis prototype of the redesigned X7 makes a particularly emphatic statement. Not only does it come with M Yellow Lights inspired by the Bavarian automaker's endurance racing programs, but four exhaust pipes further mean the 4.4-liter S68 engine is also featured.\nPreviously restricted to special editions like the M4 CS, yellow daytime running lights recently expanded to series-production models like the M350 xDrive, X5 M60, and the X5 M60e. Lesser versions of the 3 Series and X5 use white DRLs instead. But why did BMW decide on yellow for these particular models?\nYou can thank endurance racing and physics for that. Yellow light has longer wavelengths, meaning that less glare is created when reflecting off mist, fog, rain, or dust. Yellow further improves depth perception and contrast during night stints, namely the stints where the big errors are usually made in endurance racing.\nThe motorsport connection is not singular, though. French cars used yellow headlights from the 1930s all the way until the 1990s for better performance in bad weather. In real-world driving scenarios, yellow glass or bulb coatings filter out the shorter wavelengths of light, decreasing glare during night driving.\nOut back, we can see this X7 M60 xDrive packing thin and stacked horizontal taillights. Due to camouflage wrap, it's hard to determine if the slim taillight clusters are connected by a full-width light bar. Their lighting signature, however, mirrors that of the iX7 our spy photographers recently spotted at the Ring.\nPhoto: SH Proshots Gifted with a slightly more aggressive rear bumper due to the quad exhaust setup, the X7 continues with split headlights. Just like the iX7 , which uses the same codename as the next generation of the X7, the main headlamps feature lower separate housings. The daytime running lights are very slim, located on either side of the nose, where the front bumper meets the hood and front quarter panels.\nEquipped with larger wheels and brake calipers than the recently spied iX7, the X7 in M60 xDrive specification doesn't have pull-up door handles. A quarter of small winglets are featured, and this design is certain to be changed to pull-up handles in China. Under standard GB 48001-2026, automakers are required to offer a hand-operable gripping recess of at least 60 by 20 by 25 millimeters.\nNot chasing the X6's sloping coupe-inspired proportions, the boxy X7 should make in excess of 500 horsepower when optioned with the S68 engine. For reference, the outgoing M60 xDrive develops 523 hp and 553 lb-ft (750 Nm) with premium-grade dinosaur brew in the fuel tank. Modest bumps are likely, and the emphasis falls on 'modest' due to Euro 7 regulations on the Old Continent.\nAs ever, an 8-speed automatic from ZF should be the only transmission available, regardless of engine. Instead of a full-on M version of the 2028 model year X7, the G67 will gain a bunch of Alpina versions. Codenamed G69, the Alpina range is expected to include the X7 40 xDrive, X7 60 xDrive, and iX7 100 xDrive.\nAlpina models are said to default to 6 seats. Production, meanwhile, should kick off at the Spartanburg plant in the US at some point in September 2027.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nbmw x7 2028 BMW X7 BMW spyshots design spy shots\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Honda Takes Aim at New Riders with Brand-New CBR500R Four Supersport Bike",
